VERDICT

Correct: your code correctly finds the maximum row sum using a standard nested loop with optimal time and space.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You initialized max_wealth to 0, which correctly handles non-negative bank balances.

You reset sum to 0 at the start of each outer loop iteration so each customer's balance is calculated independently.

You correctly compared and updated max_wealth after checking all accounts for each customer.

Your code does not mutate the input array and uses no unnecessary memory.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs or missed edge cases.

Minor type mismatch: accounts.size() and accounts[i].size() return size_t (an unsigned type), while i and j are signed int. This can trigger compiler sign-comparison warnings.

Edge cases handled properly: a single customer with one bank account (e.g., [[10]]), customers with identical total wealth, and accounts containing zeros.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(m * n) where m is the number of customers and n is the number of bank accounts per customer, because the nested loops visit every cell in the grid exactly once.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because you only use two scalar integer variables (max_wealth and sum).

Why my approach works:

Imagine each row is a customer and each column in that row is a piggy bank. Your code opens one customer's piggy banks one by one, adds all their money together, and writes down the total. If that total is larger than the highest number seen so far, you remember it. Then you move to the next customer and repeat.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Classic Nested Loops with Indexing (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Canonical beginner baseline found in introductory programming textbooks and LeetCode editorial Approach 1.

Idea:

Use standard index-based for loops to iterate through each row i, compute the sum of its elements across columns j, and compare the sum against a running maximum.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int maximumWealth(vector<vector<int>>& accounts) {
        int max_wealth = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < accounts.size(); i++) {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < accounts[i].size(); j++) {
                sum += accounts[i][j];
            }
            if (sum > max_wealth) {
                max_wealth = sum;
            }
        }
        return max_wealth;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(m * n) because it visits all m * n elements in the matrix.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because it only keeps tracker variables.

What makes the next approach better:

Index variables i and j add boilerplate code and risk indexing bugs. C++ range-based for loops eliminate indices and make the code cleaner and less error-prone.

Approach 2: Range-Based For Loops with std::max

Where it comes from:

Modern idiomatic C++ (C++11 and later) featured in top LeetCode community solutions.

Idea:

Loop directly over each row (customer) and each number (balance) in that row. Use std::max to update the maximum wealth in one readable step.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int maximumWealth(vector<vector<int>>& accounts) {
        int maxWealth = 0;
        for (const auto& customer : accounts) {
            int currentWealth = 0;
            for (int balance : customer) {
                currentWealth += balance;
            }
            maxWealth = max(maxWealth, currentWealth);
        }
        return maxWealth;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(m * n) because every bank balance across all rows is visited once.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because only constant scalar variables are used.

What makes the next approach better:

Writing an explicit inner loop to add numbers is standard, but the C++ Standard Template Library already provides std::accumulate to sum a collection directly.

Approach 3: Standard Library std::accumulate

Where it comes from:

Standard C++ STL algorithm pattern (from header numeric) widely shown in LeetCode discussion forums.

Idea:

For each row, use std::accumulate to calculate the total sum of the row in a single line, then update maxWealth using std::max.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int maximumWealth(vector<vector<int>>& accounts) {
        int maxWealth = 0;
        for (const auto& customer : accounts) {
            int currentWealth = accumulate(customer.begin(), customer.end(), 0);
            maxWealth = max(maxWealth, currentWealth);
        }
        return maxWealth;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(m * n) because accumulate iterates through each element of each row.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space as no additional memory structures are created.

What makes the next approach better:

While Approach 3 is already optimal, some interviewers or functional programming enthusiasts prefer a purely declarative approach using transform and reduce (or std::transform_reduce).

Approach 4: Functional STL transform_reduce

Where it comes from:

Modern functional C++ (C++17 and later) using standard algorithms.

Idea:

Transform each row into its sum using std::accumulate, and simultaneously reduce all row sums by picking the maximum.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int maximumWealth(vector<vector<int>>& accounts) {
        return transform_reduce(
            accounts.begin(), accounts.end(), 0,
            [](int a, int b) { return max(a, b); },
            [](const vector<int>& customer) {
                return accumulate(customer.begin(), customer.end(), 0);
            }
        );
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(m * n) because it computes the sum of each row and finds the overall maximum.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 1 (Classic Nested Loops with Indexing). It has optimal asymptotic time and space complexity.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 2 (Range-based for loops with std::max) or Approach 3 (using std::accumulate). They demonstrate clean, modern C++ style without indexing clutter, while keeping O(1) auxiliary space.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What if each row could have a different number of bank accounts (a jagged matrix)?

Answer: Both range-based loops and std::accumulate automatically handle rows of different lengths because they rely on each row's own begin/end bounds rather than a fixed grid dimension.

Question 2: What if the wealth numbers are very large and can cause 32-bit integer overflow?

Answer: Change the running sum and max wealth variables to 64-bit integers (long long) and call accumulate with 0LL instead of 0 to avoid overflow during summation.

Question 3: What if we also need to return the index of the richest customer?

Answer: Keep a variable richestCustomerIndex alongside maxWealth. Update richestCustomerIndex = i whenever currentWealth > maxWealth.

Question 4: How would you parallelize this if there were millions of customers?

Answer: Each customer's total wealth is completely independent of every other customer. We can divide rows across multiple CPU threads using OpenMP or C++17 parallel execution policies (std::execution::par) to sum chunks in parallel and perform a reduction for the maximum.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Finding the richest customer is simply finding the maximum row sum across a 2D matrix.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Prefer range-based for loops (for (const auto& row : grid)) over index loops to avoid bounds and type signedness warnings.

Use std::max(a, b) instead of manual if (b > a) a = b statements.

Remember std::accumulate(begin, end, 0) for quickly summing container ranges.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

2D Array Traversal and Row-wise Aggregation / Reduction.

